WEBVTT

00:00:00.550 --> 00:00:03.225
Welcome to the On-Chain Operator Program.

00:00:03.225 --> 00:00:04.612
This is the long version

00:00:04.612 --> 00:00:06.300
of how the program works.

00:00:06.300 --> 00:00:09.461
Over the next twenty-odd minutes, I'll take you inside

00:00:09.461 --> 00:00:11.875
all of it: why it exists, how it's built,

00:00:11.875 --> 00:00:14.691
every module in order, the tools you'll use, and

00:00:14.691 --> 00:00:16.715
how to get the most out of it.

00:00:16.715 --> 00:00:17.950
Get comfortable.

00:00:17.950 --> 00:00:20.215
This is the map for everything that follows.

00:00:21.515 --> 00:00:22.805
Here's the plan.

00:00:22.805 --> 00:00:24.657
First, why this program exists,

00:00:24.657 --> 00:00:26.520
and the mindset it teaches.

00:00:26.520 --> 00:00:30.911
Second, how it's built: stages, modules, lessons and starters.

00:00:30.911 --> 00:00:33.160
Third, a walk through all 15 modules,

00:00:33.160 --> 00:00:35.886
with the pictures you'll see inside them.

00:00:35.886 --> 00:00:37.464
And fourth, your tools, your

00:00:37.464 --> 00:00:39.096
capstones, and how to succeed.

00:00:40.396 --> 00:00:42.971
Let's start with why this program exists.

00:00:42.971 --> 00:00:44.621
Most people get hurt in DeFi

00:00:44.621 --> 00:00:46.172
in the same four ways.

00:00:46.172 --> 00:00:49.214
Setup mistakes that can't be undone, like the wrong

00:00:49.214 --> 00:00:52.497
network, a leaked seed phrase, or one bad approval.

00:00:52.497 --> 00:00:55.976
Yields nobody can explain, until the subsidy stops.

00:00:55.976 --> 00:00:57.886
Borrowing with no buffer, so one

00:00:57.886 --> 00:01:00.211
sharp move liquidates the position.

00:01:00.211 --> 00:01:01.786
And no plan for the way out.

00:01:03.086 --> 00:01:05.451
And on-chain, there's no help desk.

00:01:05.451 --> 00:01:06.966
No chargebacks.

00:01:06.966 --> 00:01:08.541
No margin calls.

00:01:08.541 --> 00:01:09.916
No one to phone.

00:01:09.916 --> 00:01:11.999
The only protection you have is the

00:01:11.999 --> 00:01:14.266
process you follow before you act.

00:01:14.266 --> 00:01:16.871
That process is what this program teaches.

00:01:18.171 --> 00:01:19.326
Here's why.

00:01:19.326 --> 00:01:21.371
A bank keeps a private ledger, and

00:01:21.371 --> 00:01:23.716
it can correct or reverse an entry.

00:01:23.716 --> 00:01:26.023
A blockchain is a shared ledger that

00:01:26.023 --> 00:01:29.316
thousands of computers keep identical copies of.

00:01:29.316 --> 00:01:31.642
That's what makes it trustworthy, and it's

00:01:31.642 --> 00:01:33.456
also why a mistake is final.

00:01:33.456 --> 00:01:35.562
You'll meet this idea in the very first

00:01:35.562 --> 00:01:37.931
lesson, because everything else builds on it.

00:01:39.231 --> 00:01:41.656
Most people enter DeFi through a number.

00:01:41.656 --> 00:01:46.316
12% APY. Operators start with a different question.

00:01:46.316 --> 00:01:47.861
What am I being paid to risk?

00:01:49.161 --> 00:01:51.316
The operator mindset comes down to

00:01:51.316 --> 00:01:54.386
three questions, asked before every position.

00:01:54.386 --> 00:01:56.351
What am I being paid to risk?

00:01:56.351 --> 00:01:58.666
How do I get out, and how fast?

00:01:58.666 --> 00:02:00.453
And how big should this be, so that

00:02:00.453 --> 00:02:02.710
if I'm wrong, it doesn't matter much?

00:02:02.710 --> 00:02:04.766
Every lesson in this program trains

00:02:04.766 --> 00:02:06.422
one of those three questions.

00:02:07.722 --> 00:02:09.797
Here's the first answer you'll learn.

00:02:09.797 --> 00:02:13.276
Every DeFi return comes from one of five places.

00:02:13.276 --> 00:02:17.206
Service fees, like swap fees paid to liquidity providers.

00:02:17.206 --> 00:02:19.325
Interest, paid by borrowers.

00:02:19.325 --> 00:02:21.575
Security rewards, for staking.

00:02:21.575 --> 00:02:24.452
Structural carry, like funding and basis.

00:02:24.452 --> 00:02:26.138
And incentives, which are only

00:02:26.138 --> 00:02:27.907
real once you've sold them.

00:02:27.907 --> 00:02:30.342
Price going up is not a strategy.

00:02:30.342 --> 00:02:32.408
If you can't name which of these five is

00:02:32.408 --> 00:02:34.928
paying you, you don't understand the position yet.

00:02:36.229 --> 00:02:37.744
And here's the second.

00:02:37.744 --> 00:02:39.804
Every position sits on a stack.

00:02:39.804 --> 00:02:41.489
The chain that settles it.

00:02:41.489 --> 00:02:43.144
The wallet that signs it.

00:02:43.144 --> 00:02:44.589
The app that runs it.

00:02:44.589 --> 00:02:46.264
And the position itself.

00:02:46.264 --> 00:02:48.600
Each layer can fail, and every yield is a

00:02:48.600 --> 00:02:51.124
payment for taking on some of that risk.

00:02:51.124 --> 00:02:52.273
Your job is to name

00:02:52.273 --> 00:02:53.939
that risk before you deposit.

00:02:55.239 --> 00:02:57.294
Now, how the program is built.

00:02:57.294 --> 00:02:59.169
It runs in six stages.

00:02:59.169 --> 00:03:01.314
Zero, you get set up safely.

00:03:01.314 --> 00:03:03.642
Foundations, you protect a wallet and

00:03:03.642 --> 00:03:06.209
learn to trade and provide liquidity.

00:03:06.209 --> 00:03:10.429
Practitioner, you borrow, earn yield and map infrastructure risk.

00:03:10.429 --> 00:03:14.374
Analyst, you research any protocol and read on-chain data.

00:03:14.374 --> 00:03:18.204
Strategist, thirty strategies, hedging and stress tests.

00:03:18.204 --> 00:03:20.769
And Operator, you run your own on-chain bank.

00:03:22.069 --> 00:03:25.376
In numbers: 15 modules, 107 lessons, plus

00:03:25.376 --> 00:03:27.896
a Mastery Starter at the top of

00:03:27.896 --> 00:03:31.045
every module, and 30 strategy playbooks.

00:03:32.345 --> 00:03:34.420
The stages unlock in order.

00:03:34.420 --> 00:03:36.560
You move on by passing the previous

00:03:36.560 --> 00:03:39.060
stage's quizzes, not by the calendar.

00:03:39.060 --> 00:03:40.375
That's deliberate.

00:03:40.375 --> 00:03:44.295
It means nobody reaches leverage before they've mastered safety.

00:03:44.295 --> 00:03:45.740
Go at your own pace.

00:03:45.740 --> 00:03:48.720
There's no deadline, and you keep lifetime access.

00:03:50.020 --> 00:03:52.118
Every lesson has the same five parts,

00:03:52.118 --> 00:03:54.240
so you always know where you are.

00:03:54.240 --> 00:03:55.980
It starts with an objective: what

00:03:55.980 --> 00:03:57.355
you'll be able to do.

00:03:57.355 --> 00:04:00.199
Then an explanation, in plain words, with every

00:04:00.199 --> 00:04:02.810
term defined the first time it appears.

00:04:02.810 --> 00:04:04.370
Then a worked example, with

00:04:04.370 --> 00:04:06.565
every number calculated on screen.

00:04:06.565 --> 00:04:08.540
Then a checklist, which you do for

00:04:08.540 --> 00:04:10.590
real, with a small test amount.

00:04:10.590 --> 00:04:12.752
And finally, a three-question quiz.

00:04:14.052 --> 00:04:16.869
And every module opens with a Mastery Starter,

00:04:16.869 --> 00:04:19.387
so you can start any topic from zero.

00:04:19.387 --> 00:04:22.412
It has a 60-second version of the whole module.

00:04:22.412 --> 00:04:23.857
The words you'll need.

00:04:23.857 --> 00:04:26.102
What to have in place before you start.

00:04:26.102 --> 00:04:28.507
One safe first step you can take today.

00:04:28.507 --> 00:04:31.712
A ladder from beginner, to practitioner, to master.

00:04:31.712 --> 00:04:32.760
And a clear test for

00:04:32.760 --> 00:04:34.357
when you've mastered the module.

00:04:35.657 --> 00:04:39.442
Most modules also have expert lessons, 15 in all.

00:04:39.442 --> 00:04:42.807
They show how the machinery really works underneath:

00:04:42.807 --> 00:04:46.235
how automated market makers are designed, how lending

00:04:46.235 --> 00:04:49.282
protocols handle bad debt, how to read contract

00:04:49.282 --> 00:04:51.487
code, how to value a protocol.

00:04:51.487 --> 00:04:53.682
You don't need them to operate safely.

00:04:53.682 --> 00:04:55.647
They're there when you want to go deeper.

00:04:56.947 --> 00:04:58.302
Module 0.

00:04:58.302 --> 00:04:59.537
Crypto From Zero.

00:05:00.837 --> 00:05:04.102
Module 0 assumes you've never owned crypto.

00:05:04.102 --> 00:05:06.788
Its seven steps take you from nothing to set

00:05:06.788 --> 00:05:09.653
up: secure your accounts, open and lock down an

00:05:09.653 --> 00:05:12.637
exchange, make a first small purchase, set up and

00:05:12.637 --> 00:05:15.203
back up a wallet, send a first transfer on

00:05:15.203 --> 00:05:18.247
the right network, practise on a test network, and

00:05:18.247 --> 00:05:20.457
finish with a security baseline.

00:05:20.457 --> 00:05:23.102
It all comes with a printable Day-1 Setup Kit.

00:05:24.402 --> 00:05:26.792
Here's a Module 0 example.

00:05:26.792 --> 00:05:29.357
The price on the chart isn't what you pay.

00:05:29.357 --> 00:05:31.087
You lose a little to the spread, a

00:05:31.087 --> 00:05:33.064
little to the trading fee, and a little

00:05:33.064 --> 00:05:35.242
more to the network when you withdraw.

00:05:35.242 --> 00:05:36.957
So you'll compare offers by what

00:05:36.957 --> 00:05:38.517
actually lands in your wallet.

00:05:39.817 --> 00:05:41.456
One of the first ideas you'll

00:05:41.456 --> 00:05:43.232
meet is who holds the keys.

00:05:43.232 --> 00:05:46.058
On an exchange, the exchange holds them, which

00:05:46.058 --> 00:05:48.462
is fine for buying and cashing out.

00:05:48.462 --> 00:05:50.811
In your own wallet, you hold them, which

00:05:50.811 --> 00:05:52.758
is what you need for DeFi, and it

00:05:52.758 --> 00:05:55.407
means your backup is your responsibility.

00:05:55.407 --> 00:05:57.659
You'll test that backup by restoring it,

00:05:57.659 --> 00:05:59.142
before you ever rely on it.

00:06:00.442 --> 00:06:01.707
Module 1.

00:06:01.707 --> 00:06:03.182
Foundations & Safety.

00:06:04.482 --> 00:06:07.177
Module 1 is foundations and safety.

00:06:07.177 --> 00:06:09.923
You'll learn what really happens when you press

00:06:09.923 --> 00:06:12.783
send: you sign, the transaction waits publicly in

00:06:12.783 --> 00:06:14.957
the mempool, a validator puts it in a

00:06:14.957 --> 00:06:16.802
block, and then it's final.

00:06:16.802 --> 00:06:18.498
You'll learn why gas is paid

00:06:18.498 --> 00:06:20.253
even when a transaction fails.

00:06:21.553 --> 00:06:22.883
Then the attacks.

00:06:22.883 --> 00:06:25.678
Fake sites that look exactly like the real one.

00:06:25.678 --> 00:06:29.058
Drainers, where a single signature hands over everything.

00:06:29.058 --> 00:06:32.083
Fake support staff who ask for your seed phrase.

00:06:32.083 --> 00:06:34.983
And poisoned addresses planted in your history.

00:06:34.983 --> 00:06:37.386
You'll learn to recognise all four, and to

00:06:37.386 --> 00:06:39.957
simulate a transaction before you sign it, so

00:06:39.957 --> 00:06:41.633
you know exactly what it does.

00:06:42.933 --> 00:06:45.308
And you'll read every approval before you

00:06:45.308 --> 00:06:47.627
sign it: which token, which contract can

00:06:47.627 --> 00:06:49.228
spend it, and how much.

00:06:49.228 --> 00:06:52.468
An infinite approval stays open after the trade.

00:06:52.468 --> 00:06:54.903
An exact one covers only this trade.

00:06:54.903 --> 00:06:56.456
You'll default to exact, and

00:06:56.456 --> 00:06:57.848
revoke what you don't use.

00:06:59.148 --> 00:07:01.304
You'll also learn how stablecoins are

00:07:01.304 --> 00:07:03.533
built, and how each design breaks.

00:07:03.533 --> 00:07:06.678
Fiat-backed coins depend on an issuer's reserves.

00:07:06.678 --> 00:07:10.477
Crypto-backed coins depend on collateral and liquidations.

00:07:10.477 --> 00:07:13.415
And algorithmic designs depend on confidence,

00:07:13.415 --> 00:07:15.012
which can vanish in days.

00:07:16.312 --> 00:07:17.577
Module 2.

00:07:17.577 --> 00:07:18.657
Trading On-Chain.

00:07:19.957 --> 00:07:22.302
Module 2 is trading on-chain.

00:07:22.302 --> 00:07:23.659
It starts with the maths

00:07:23.659 --> 00:07:25.912
behind every automated market maker.

00:07:25.912 --> 00:07:28.510
In this example, a pool

00:07:28.510 --> 00:07:31.732
holds 100 ETH and $300,000.

00:07:31.732 --> 00:07:39.972
Swapping in 10 ETH returns about $27,273, not $30,000.

00:07:39.972 --> 00:07:41.939
That's price impact, and you'll learn

00:07:41.939 --> 00:07:43.647
to calculate it before you trade.

00:07:44.947 --> 00:07:46.732
Then providing liquidity.

00:07:46.732 --> 00:07:49.016
You deposit two assets, earn a share

00:07:49.016 --> 00:07:51.177
of every swap's fees, and your mix

00:07:51.177 --> 00:07:53.452
of assets shifts as prices move.

00:07:53.452 --> 00:07:55.860
Your real result is the fees you earned,

00:07:55.860 --> 00:07:59.507
minus the impermanent loss, compared with simply holding.

00:07:59.507 --> 00:08:01.558
You'll learn to measure that honestly, and

00:08:01.558 --> 00:08:02.942
when it's worth doing at all.

00:08:04.242 --> 00:08:06.557
Here's impermanent loss as a chart.

00:08:06.557 --> 00:08:09.796
If the price doubles, or halves, a 50/50

00:08:09.796 --> 00:08:13.967
pool leaves you about 5.7% behind simply holding.

00:08:13.967 --> 00:08:16.687
If it triples, 13.4%.

00:08:16.687 --> 00:08:18.437
Your fees have to beat this line,

00:08:18.437 --> 00:08:20.187
or holding would have been better.

00:08:21.487 --> 00:08:22.782
And execution.

00:08:22.782 --> 00:08:26.361
Market orders, limit orders, orders split over time, and

00:08:26.361 --> 00:08:30.052
intent-based orders where solvers compete to fill you.

00:08:30.052 --> 00:08:32.318
Each one is safer in different situations,

00:08:32.318 --> 00:08:34.162
and you'll learn which to use when.

00:08:35.462 --> 00:08:36.767
Module 3.

00:08:36.767 --> 00:08:38.122
Lending & Leverage.

00:08:39.422 --> 00:08:42.150
Module 3 is lending and leverage.

00:08:42.150 --> 00:08:45.608
You'll see how a lending pool works: suppliers deposit,

00:08:45.608 --> 00:08:49.128
borrowers post collateral and pay interest, and the rate

00:08:49.128 --> 00:08:52.145
rises with how much of the pool is borrowed.

00:08:52.145 --> 00:08:55.172
Near full utilisation, rates jump, and lenders

00:08:55.172 --> 00:08:56.975
may not be able to withdraw.

00:08:58.275 --> 00:09:01.130
The centre of this module is the health factor.

00:09:01.130 --> 00:09:05.847
10 ETH at $3,000, with $12,000 borrowed, gives a

00:09:05.847 --> 00:09:10.575
health factor of 2, and liquidation at $1,500.

00:09:10.575 --> 00:09:15.350
Borrow $16,000 instead, and liquidation moves up to $2,000.

00:09:15.350 --> 00:09:17.503
You'll draw this line before every borrow,

00:09:17.503 --> 00:09:18.805
for the rest of your life.

00:09:20.105 --> 00:09:23.578
You'll also learn perpetual futures: longs and shorts

00:09:23.578 --> 00:09:26.665
with no expiry, funding payments that keep them

00:09:26.665 --> 00:09:29.345
near the spot price, and liquidation.

00:09:29.345 --> 00:09:31.922
At 20× leverage, a move of under

00:09:31.922 --> 00:09:34.330
5% can wipe out the margin.

00:09:34.330 --> 00:09:36.259
That's why you'll use perps to hedge

00:09:36.259 --> 00:09:37.980
and to earn carry, not to gamble.

00:09:39.280 --> 00:09:41.475
And you'll learn why falls accelerate.

00:09:41.475 --> 00:09:45.008
Liquidations sell collateral into a falling market, which

00:09:45.008 --> 00:09:48.597
pushes prices lower, which triggers more liquidations.

00:09:48.597 --> 00:09:50.491
Your buffer is your time to react,

00:09:50.491 --> 00:09:52.763
and you'll write your defence plan before

00:09:52.763 --> 00:09:54.549
you borrow, not during the crash.

00:09:55.849 --> 00:09:57.159
Module 4.

00:09:57.159 --> 00:09:57.779
Yield.

00:09:59.079 --> 00:10:00.844
Module 4 is yield.

00:10:00.844 --> 00:10:03.329
The core skill is splitting any APY

00:10:03.329 --> 00:10:06.529
into what's organic and what's subsidised.

00:10:06.529 --> 00:10:08.659
Here, Pool A shows 20%, but

00:10:08.659 --> 00:10:10.633
16 of it is emissions.

00:10:10.633 --> 00:10:12.313
Pool B shows 9, and 8

00:10:12.313 --> 00:10:14.064
of it is real fees.

00:10:14.064 --> 00:10:16.279
You'll learn which part lasts, and which

00:10:16.279 --> 00:10:18.494
part disappears when the incentives stop.

00:10:19.794 --> 00:10:21.049
Then staking.

00:10:21.049 --> 00:10:24.560
Native staking, liquid staking tokens that accrue

00:10:24.560 --> 00:10:27.087
rewards but can trade below the ETH

00:10:27.087 --> 00:10:30.247
behind them, and restaking, which adds yield

00:10:30.247 --> 00:10:32.494
and adds new ways to be slashed.

00:10:33.794 --> 00:10:35.529
And real-world assets.

00:10:35.529 --> 00:10:38.192
When you buy a tokenized treasury, you own

00:10:38.192 --> 00:10:41.102
a claim on an issuer, who promises redemption,

00:10:41.102 --> 00:10:44.189
through a custodian who holds the real asset.

00:10:44.189 --> 00:10:45.729
You'll learn to see every link in

00:10:45.729 --> 00:10:47.134
that chain before you trust it.

00:10:48.434 --> 00:10:49.799
Module 5.

00:10:49.799 --> 00:10:51.024
Infrastructure Risk.

00:10:52.324 --> 00:10:56.069
Module 5 is infrastructure risk: everything a position

00:10:56.069 --> 00:10:59.229
depends on that isn't the position itself.

00:10:59.229 --> 00:11:00.594
Bridges first.

00:11:00.594 --> 00:11:03.028
Some depend on a small set of signers,

00:11:03.028 --> 00:11:05.774
some on the chains' own consensus, and some

00:11:05.774 --> 00:11:08.009
on at least one honest watcher.

00:11:08.009 --> 00:11:10.174
You'll size your exposure to the trust model.

00:11:11.474 --> 00:11:13.567
Then oracles, the price feeds that

00:11:13.567 --> 00:11:16.079
decide when positions get liquidated.

00:11:16.079 --> 00:11:17.603
A single spot price can be

00:11:17.603 --> 00:11:19.539
pushed around in a thin pool.

00:11:19.539 --> 00:11:21.770
A time-weighted average is harder to

00:11:21.770 --> 00:11:23.999
manipulate, but lags fast moves.

00:11:23.999 --> 00:11:26.669
You'll always ask which price secures your position.

00:11:27.969 --> 00:11:29.414
And the code itself.

00:11:29.414 --> 00:11:31.940
Many contracts can be upgraded after you

00:11:31.940 --> 00:11:34.704
deposit, by whoever holds the admin key.

00:11:34.704 --> 00:11:37.414
You'll learn to check the timelock and the admin

00:11:37.414 --> 00:11:39.958
before you put money in, and to read verified

00:11:39.958 --> 00:11:41.839
code well enough to check a claim.

00:11:43.139 --> 00:11:44.479
Module 6.

00:11:44.479 --> 00:11:45.759
Protocol Research.

00:11:47.059 --> 00:11:49.684
Module 6 is protocol research.

00:11:49.684 --> 00:11:51.087
You'll learn a six-step loop

00:11:51.087 --> 00:11:52.549
you can run on anything.

00:11:52.549 --> 00:11:54.424
Mechanism: how it works.

00:11:54.424 --> 00:11:56.789
Cash flow: where the money comes from.

00:11:56.789 --> 00:11:58.049
Dependencies.

00:11:58.049 --> 00:11:59.209
Solvency.

00:11:59.209 --> 00:12:00.219
Evidence.

00:12:00.219 --> 00:12:01.269
And exit.

00:12:01.269 --> 00:12:03.594
You'll finish with a full due-diligence

00:12:03.594 --> 00:12:04.989
file on a real protocol.

00:12:06.289 --> 00:12:10.009
You'll learn tokenomics: the difference between the supply

00:12:10.009 --> 00:12:12.972
trading today and the fully diluted value, how

00:12:12.972 --> 00:12:16.313
large unlocks add supply, and whether the protocol's

00:12:16.313 --> 00:12:18.709
success ever reaches the token at all.

00:12:20.009 --> 00:12:21.923
And you'll study how DeFi failures

00:12:21.923 --> 00:12:24.192
happened, through real case studies.

00:12:24.192 --> 00:12:25.212
They rhyme.

00:12:25.212 --> 00:12:26.979
Manipulated oracles.

00:12:26.979 --> 00:12:28.234
Bad upgrades.

00:12:28.234 --> 00:12:29.419
Bank runs.

00:12:29.419 --> 00:12:31.241
And incentive programmes that end,

00:12:31.241 --> 00:12:33.154
taking the liquidity with them.

00:12:33.154 --> 00:12:35.459
Learn the patterns, and you'll spot them early.

00:12:36.758 --> 00:12:38.128
Module 7.

00:12:38.128 --> 00:12:39.383
On-Chain Analytics.

00:12:40.683 --> 00:12:42.851
Module 7 is on-chain analytics,

00:12:42.851 --> 00:12:45.048
and it starts with humility.

00:12:45.048 --> 00:12:46.733
Volume can be faked.

00:12:46.733 --> 00:12:48.448
Addresses aren't people.

00:12:48.448 --> 00:12:50.632
And total value locked counts capital

00:12:50.632 --> 00:12:52.887
that's only there for the rewards.

00:12:52.887 --> 00:12:54.702
Every metric gets its caveat.

00:12:56.002 --> 00:12:59.513
You'll master the block explorer: the transaction hash, who

00:12:59.513 --> 00:13:02.672
signed and which contract was called, the event logs,

00:13:02.672 --> 00:13:06.132
and the internal calls that show what really happened.

00:13:06.132 --> 00:13:07.824
You won't need an app's interface

00:13:07.824 --> 00:13:09.367
to know what a transaction did.

00:13:10.667 --> 00:13:12.282
And you'll read markets.

00:13:12.282 --> 00:13:14.531
Here, a trade that's 1% of a

00:13:14.531 --> 00:13:17.932
pool's reserve moves the price about 1%.

00:13:17.932 --> 00:13:20.512
At 10%, it moves it over 9.

00:13:20.512 --> 00:13:22.601
You'll check whether a pool can take your

00:13:22.601 --> 00:13:24.639
trade before you place it, and query the

00:13:24.639 --> 00:13:26.877
chain yourself when a dashboard isn't enough.

00:13:28.177 --> 00:13:29.442
Module 8.

00:13:29.442 --> 00:13:31.217
The DeFi Operating System.

00:13:32.517 --> 00:13:35.222
Module 8 turns knowledge into a system.

00:13:35.222 --> 00:13:37.978
You'll build a portfolio in buckets: a core

00:13:37.978 --> 00:13:41.735
of simple, well-tested positions, a satellite of strategies

00:13:41.735 --> 00:13:45.417
you've tested, and a small, capped speculative bucket.

00:13:45.417 --> 00:13:47.589
Each bucket, and each position inside

00:13:47.589 --> 00:13:48.847
it, has a written cap.

00:13:50.147 --> 00:13:53.055
You'll keep a risk register: every position, its

00:13:53.055 --> 00:13:55.669
risks, a score, and a written response.

00:13:55.669 --> 00:13:58.634
Then an operating playbook with the same four

00:13:58.634 --> 00:14:02.244
phases every time: deploy, monitor, respond, and review.

00:14:03.543 --> 00:14:06.743
And you'll size positions from numbers, not feelings.

00:14:06.743 --> 00:14:09.242
This chart shows one-day value at

00:14:09.242 --> 00:14:11.673
risk for a $100,000 position.

00:14:11.673 --> 00:14:15.085
At 70% volatility, a bad day at

00:14:15.085 --> 00:14:18.583
the 95% level is about $6,040.

00:14:18.583 --> 00:14:20.816
And because crypto has fatter tails than

00:14:20.816 --> 00:14:22.940
this model assumes, you'll treat it as

00:14:22.940 --> 00:14:24.193
a floor, not a ceiling.

00:14:25.493 --> 00:14:28.109
Inside Module 8 is the strategy

00:14:28.109 --> 00:14:31.271
library: 30 strategies in 7 levels.

00:14:31.271 --> 00:14:33.996
Core, like stablecoin lending and staking.

00:14:33.996 --> 00:14:35.096
Liquidity.

00:14:35.096 --> 00:14:36.016
Yield.

00:14:36.016 --> 00:14:39.041
Advanced, like leveraged loops and funding carry.

00:14:39.041 --> 00:14:40.691
Treasury and research.

00:14:40.691 --> 00:14:43.981
Professional, like fixed rates, basis and options.

00:14:43.981 --> 00:14:45.166
And Expert.

00:14:45.166 --> 00:14:47.854
Every strategy shows where its return comes

00:14:47.854 --> 00:14:50.114
from, the maths, the steps, the kill

00:14:50.114 --> 00:14:52.436
rules, and exactly how it loses money.

00:14:53.736 --> 00:14:55.996
Here's an example from the library.

00:14:55.996 --> 00:14:59.656
A 3× loop on 3.5% collateral yield.

00:14:59.656 --> 00:15:03.726
At a 2.5% borrow rate, you net 5.5%.

00:15:03.726 --> 00:15:07.336
Above 3.5% borrow, leverage adds nothing.

00:15:07.336 --> 00:15:10.531
And at 5.25%, the return is wiped out.

00:15:10.531 --> 00:15:12.428
The spread is everything, and you'll

00:15:12.428 --> 00:15:14.326
know the break-even before you start.

00:15:15.626 --> 00:15:16.941
Module 9.

00:15:16.941 --> 00:15:18.606
DeFi vs Grid Bots.

00:15:19.906 --> 00:15:23.071
Module 9 connects DeFi to grid bots.

00:15:23.071 --> 00:15:26.737
A concentrated liquidity position and a grid bot do

00:15:26.737 --> 00:15:29.838
almost the same job: both sell as the price

00:15:29.838 --> 00:15:33.099
rises and buy as it falls, inside a range.

00:15:33.099 --> 00:15:35.797
The difference is the risks: fees, custody, and

00:15:35.797 --> 00:15:38.326
what happens when the price leaves the range.

00:15:39.626 --> 00:15:41.421
You'll learn when each one wins.

00:15:41.421 --> 00:15:43.346
In a range, both can work.

00:15:43.346 --> 00:15:45.706
In a strong trend, neither does well.

00:15:45.706 --> 00:15:47.826
And you'll build one system with two

00:15:47.826 --> 00:15:50.117
sleeves, an exchange grid sleeve and an

00:15:50.117 --> 00:15:52.466
on-chain sleeve, under a single risk cap.

00:15:53.766 --> 00:15:55.071
Module 10.

00:15:55.071 --> 00:15:56.586
Advanced Yield Engineering.

00:15:57.886 --> 00:16:00.996
Module 10 is advanced yield engineering.

00:16:00.996 --> 00:16:02.631
Fixed-rate yield first.

00:16:02.631 --> 00:16:05.747
A principal token bought at a discount converges to

00:16:05.747 --> 00:16:08.444
its full value at maturity, which locks in a

00:16:08.444 --> 00:16:10.961
fixed rate if you hold it to the end.

00:16:10.961 --> 00:16:13.626
You'll learn exactly what that fixed rate is short.

00:16:14.926 --> 00:16:16.606
Then basis and carry.

00:16:16.606 --> 00:16:19.355
Buy spot, short a dated future, and capture the

00:16:19.355 --> 00:16:22.061
gap between them, without a view on price.

00:16:22.061 --> 00:16:24.304
It works only if both legs are held

00:16:24.304 --> 00:16:27.221
to expiry and your margin is never called.

00:16:27.221 --> 00:16:30.492
You'll also learn funding carry, options income, and when

00:16:30.492 --> 00:16:32.805
you're really being paid to be the house.

00:16:34.105 --> 00:16:35.700
And options income.

00:16:35.700 --> 00:16:37.031
A covered call earns a

00:16:37.031 --> 00:16:39.010
premium but caps your upside.

00:16:39.010 --> 00:16:41.550
A cash-secured put earns a premium, and may

00:16:41.550 --> 00:16:43.870
buy the asset for you after a fall.

00:16:43.870 --> 00:16:45.654
Every one of these strategies is

00:16:45.654 --> 00:16:47.006
taught with how it loses.

00:16:48.306 --> 00:16:49.711
Module 11.

00:16:49.711 --> 00:16:51.491
Hedging & Risk Engineering.

00:16:52.791 --> 00:16:56.086
Module 11 is hedging and risk engineering.

00:16:56.086 --> 00:16:58.575
You'll hedge the risks you don't want, with

00:16:58.575 --> 00:17:01.120
a perpetual short, which costs funding, or a

00:17:01.120 --> 00:17:03.796
put option, which costs a premium upfront.

00:17:03.796 --> 00:17:06.179
You'll learn what on-chain cover actually

00:17:06.179 --> 00:17:07.711
pays, and what it excludes.

00:17:09.011 --> 00:17:11.606
You'll protect every borrow with three layers.

00:17:11.606 --> 00:17:14.531
A buffer, keeping the health factor well above one.

00:17:14.531 --> 00:17:16.886
Alerts that warn you long before trouble.

00:17:16.886 --> 00:17:18.695
And a repayment amount worked out

00:17:18.695 --> 00:17:20.451
in advance, with the funds ready.

00:17:21.751 --> 00:17:23.816
Then you'll stress-test the whole book.

00:17:23.816 --> 00:17:25.711
A 30% price fall.

00:17:25.711 --> 00:17:27.346
A stablecoin depeg.

00:17:27.346 --> 00:17:29.001
A protocol exploit.

00:17:29.001 --> 00:17:31.295
For each one, what breaks, and how you fix

00:17:31.295 --> 00:17:33.536
it now, before the market does it for you.

00:17:34.836 --> 00:17:37.063
And you'll have an incident plan for

00:17:37.063 --> 00:17:39.229
the first 60 minutes of an exploit,

00:17:39.229 --> 00:17:41.515
a depeg, or a compromised wallet.

00:17:41.515 --> 00:17:43.350
Stop and sign nothing new.

00:17:43.350 --> 00:17:44.895
Size the exposure.

00:17:44.895 --> 00:17:47.050
Move keys and funds to safety.

00:17:47.050 --> 00:17:50.225
Then act on public facts only, and record everything.

00:17:51.525 --> 00:17:52.915
Module 12.

00:17:52.915 --> 00:17:54.370
Operate as Your Own Bank.

00:17:55.670 --> 00:17:57.110
Module 12 is where you

00:17:57.110 --> 00:17:58.975
operate as your own bank.

00:17:58.975 --> 00:18:01.045
It's a method, not a licence.

00:18:01.045 --> 00:18:04.474
Four pillars: custody, a credit line, a lending desk

00:18:04.474 --> 00:18:07.385
and a treasury, standing on a base of books,

00:18:07.385 --> 00:18:10.490
records and succession, under one income policy.

00:18:11.790 --> 00:18:13.725
You'll build a real balance sheet.

00:18:13.725 --> 00:18:15.510
Assets at market value.

00:18:15.510 --> 00:18:18.590
Liabilities, like loans and accrued interest.

00:18:18.590 --> 00:18:21.994
And the numbers that matter: equity, loan-to-value, and how

00:18:21.994 --> 00:18:24.660
many months of runway your liquid assets cover.

00:18:25.959 --> 00:18:28.294
You'll design custody in three tiers.

00:18:28.294 --> 00:18:30.569
A vault that needs more than one key.

00:18:30.569 --> 00:18:32.690
An operating wallet with limits.

00:18:32.690 --> 00:18:35.055
And a small hot float for anything new.

00:18:35.055 --> 00:18:37.439
No single lost phone, stolen key or

00:18:37.439 --> 00:18:39.625
bad signature can drain the bank.

00:18:40.925 --> 00:18:42.420
And a liquidity ladder.

00:18:42.420 --> 00:18:44.798
Using an example of $5,000 a month of

00:18:44.798 --> 00:18:48.301
spending, one month sits instantly available, five more

00:18:48.301 --> 00:18:51.054
in blue-chip lending you can reach the same

00:18:51.054 --> 00:18:53.119
day, and the next 6 to 12 months

00:18:53.119 --> 00:18:56.360
in positions that mature when you'll need them.

00:18:56.360 --> 00:18:58.005
Everything else can grow.

00:18:58.005 --> 00:19:00.490
Add a written plan so your family could recover

00:19:00.490 --> 00:19:03.285
everything if you couldn't, and you're running a bank.

00:19:04.585 --> 00:19:06.120
Module 13.

00:19:06.120 --> 00:19:07.165
The Income Engine.

00:19:08.465 --> 00:19:11.255
Module 13 is the income engine.

00:19:11.255 --> 00:19:15.027
You'll rank income by durability, not by headline yield.

00:19:15.027 --> 00:19:18.707
Staking rewards and lending interest at the durable end.

00:19:18.707 --> 00:19:20.917
Emissions and points at the fragile end.

00:19:22.218 --> 00:19:25.313
You'll subtract expected losses from every yield.

00:19:25.313 --> 00:19:28.851
An 18% farm, with a 10% yearly chance of

00:19:28.851 --> 00:19:32.603
losing 60%, has an expected yield of 12.

00:19:32.603 --> 00:19:34.426
The 40% farm barely beats

00:19:34.426 --> 00:19:36.038
it, with far more risk.

00:19:37.338 --> 00:19:39.253
And you'll set a payout policy.

00:19:39.253 --> 00:19:40.828
Start from the headline.

00:19:40.828 --> 00:19:42.853
Subtract expected losses.

00:19:42.853 --> 00:19:44.533
Keep part as a buffer.

00:19:44.533 --> 00:19:46.197
Only then decide what you may

00:19:46.197 --> 00:19:48.438
take out, and never from principal.

00:19:48.438 --> 00:19:51.078
This is an illustration, not a promise.

00:19:51.078 --> 00:19:53.298
No income is guaranteed, and the whole

00:19:53.298 --> 00:19:55.348
point is to live within what's real.

00:19:56.648 --> 00:19:58.123
Module 14.

00:19:58.123 --> 00:19:59.558
Automation & Mastery.

00:20:00.858 --> 00:20:04.048
Module 14 is automation and mastery.

00:20:04.048 --> 00:20:06.730
You'll set up monitoring, so you hear about a

00:20:06.730 --> 00:20:10.287
problem before it costs money: positions, watchers that read

00:20:10.287 --> 00:20:12.853
the chain, and alerts that reach your phone.

00:20:14.153 --> 00:20:16.928
You'll automate with the least permission possible.

00:20:16.928 --> 00:20:19.272
A keeper that does one job, inside a

00:20:19.272 --> 00:20:22.377
box of limited functions and spending caps, that

00:20:22.377 --> 00:20:24.388
you can revoke at any time.

00:20:24.388 --> 00:20:27.104
And never, ever, a handed-over seed phrase.

00:20:28.403 --> 00:20:31.243
And you'll finish with the operator capstone: your

00:20:31.243 --> 00:20:34.083
balance sheet, your policies, your monitors, and a

00:20:34.083 --> 00:20:36.443
stress test with every failure fixed.

00:20:36.443 --> 00:20:38.443
A document clear enough that a stranger

00:20:38.443 --> 00:20:39.843
could run your bank from it.

00:20:41.143 --> 00:20:42.448
Now your tools.

00:20:42.448 --> 00:20:45.133
The program comes with calculators, so every

00:20:45.133 --> 00:20:47.223
number you act on is computed.

00:20:47.223 --> 00:20:49.617
Here's the health factor calculator on

00:20:49.617 --> 00:20:51.513
the example from Module 3.

00:20:51.513 --> 00:20:56.046
10 ETH at $3,000, a liquidation threshold

00:20:56.046 --> 00:20:59.368
of 0.8, and $12,000 of debt.

00:20:59.368 --> 00:21:03.347
It returns a loan-to-value of 40%, a health factor

00:21:03.347 --> 00:21:06.768
of 2, and a liquidation price of $1,500.

00:21:06.768 --> 00:21:09.784
There are 20 calculators, from impermanent loss

00:21:09.784 --> 00:21:11.543
to income and value at risk.

00:21:12.843 --> 00:21:15.284
You'll also have 17 worksheets, for due

00:21:15.284 --> 00:21:18.758
diligence, pre-launch checks and your bank policies.

00:21:18.758 --> 00:21:21.149
The Course Hub, which puts every lesson,

00:21:21.149 --> 00:21:23.831
video, checklist and calculator in one place,

00:21:23.831 --> 00:21:26.113
with search and progress tracking.

00:21:26.113 --> 00:21:28.108
And a Day-1 Setup Kit you can print.

00:21:29.408 --> 00:21:31.013
There are two capstones.

00:21:31.013 --> 00:21:34.046
The analyst capstone, after Module 7, is a

00:21:34.046 --> 00:21:37.501
due-diligence file on a real protocol, ending in

00:21:37.501 --> 00:21:40.693
a verdict with size, conditions and exit.

00:21:40.693 --> 00:21:42.354
The operator capstone, at the

00:21:42.354 --> 00:21:44.148
end, is your personal bank.

00:21:44.148 --> 00:21:46.038
Each is marked against a written

00:21:46.038 --> 00:21:48.628
rubric, and passing earns a certificate.

00:21:48.628 --> 00:21:52.193
Certificates reflect course completion only, not a licence.

00:21:53.493 --> 00:21:55.248
Finally, how to succeed.

00:21:55.248 --> 00:21:56.923
Do the stages in order.

00:21:56.923 --> 00:21:59.948
Do every checklist for real, with small amounts.

00:21:59.948 --> 00:22:02.453
Use the calculators instead of guessing.

00:22:02.453 --> 00:22:04.533
And write things down: your thesis,

00:22:04.533 --> 00:22:06.508
your exit, and your policies.

00:22:06.508 --> 00:22:08.551
The people who get the most from this

00:22:08.551 --> 00:22:10.594
program are the ones who do the work,

00:22:10.594 --> 00:22:12.691
not the ones who watch the most videos.

00:22:13.991 --> 00:22:16.876
Let's check the most important idea in this video.

00:22:16.876 --> 00:22:18.676
Before any position, what's the

00:22:18.676 --> 00:22:20.476
first question an operator asks?

00:22:24.896 --> 00:22:27.581
The answer: what am I being paid to risk?

00:22:27.581 --> 00:22:28.798
Then how do I get out,

00:22:28.798 --> 00:22:30.226
and how big should this be?

00:22:31.526 --> 00:22:33.421
And the rules that never change.

00:22:33.421 --> 00:22:35.781
Never share your seed phrase or keys.

00:22:35.781 --> 00:22:38.836
Nobody from this program will ever ask for them.

00:22:38.836 --> 00:22:40.450
Use only money you can afford

00:22:40.450 --> 00:22:42.041
to lose while you learn.

00:22:42.041 --> 00:22:45.370
And no one, including us, can promise you returns.

00:22:45.370 --> 00:22:46.855
What you get is a process.

00:22:48.156 --> 00:22:49.881
So here's your first week.

00:22:49.881 --> 00:22:53.386
Open Module 0 and print the Day-1 Setup Kit.

00:22:53.386 --> 00:22:56.966
Secure your email with a password manager and 2FA.

00:22:56.966 --> 00:22:58.251
Work through the kit in

00:22:58.251 --> 00:22:59.783
order, over a few days.

00:22:59.783 --> 00:23:02.498
And take each lesson's quiz before moving on.

00:23:02.498 --> 00:23:04.375
By the end of the week, you'll be

00:23:04.375 --> 00:23:06.473
set up safely, and ready for Module 1.

00:23:07.773 --> 00:23:10.525
This is education, not financial advice,

00:23:10.525 --> 00:23:12.838
and no results are guaranteed.

00:23:12.838 --> 00:23:15.144
What you're getting is a process: one

00:23:15.144 --> 00:23:17.753
you can explain, position by position, for

00:23:17.753 --> 00:23:19.088
as long as you invest.

00:23:20.388 --> 00:23:22.193
That's how the program works.

00:23:22.193 --> 00:23:24.168
Welcome aboard, operator.

00:23:24.168 --> 00:23:26.503
Open Module 0, and let's begin.
